+/// Name of the isolated world [`locate_browser_ref`] reads in and a filmed
+/// click or insert runs in. Chromium keeps one world per frame and name, so
+/// repeated calls on a document share it.
+const LOCATE_WORLD: &str = "cua-driver-locate";
+
+/// Where the ref's node is, evaluated ON the node with the click sequence's
+/// own geometry: its bounding rectangle in viewport CSS px and what
+/// `elementFromPoint` finds at its centre, and at (`px`, `py`) when both are
+/// numbers.
+///
+/// `focus` is whether the node is the active element of its root (its
+/// document or shadow root) and whether its document has focus: when both
+/// hold, focusing the node again changes nothing in the page.
+///
+/// `clipped` is whether an ancestor that clips or scrolls its overflow
+/// leaves part of the rectangle outside its padding box: such a node is
+/// what `DOM.scrollIntoViewIfNeeded` and `focus()` scroll. The walk follows
+/// the flat tree (an assigned slot, then the parent, then a shadow host). A
+/// slot in a closed shadow tree is not visible to script in any world, so a
+/// clipping ancestor inside a closed tree, between a slotted node and the
+/// tree's host, is not seen: a known limit. The root element is skipped: its overflow applies to the viewport, which the
+/// rectangle and `inner` already describe. `body` is skipped only when its
+/// overflow propagates to the viewport too, that is when the root's overflow
+/// is `visible` and neither element has containment; otherwise it is a
+/// scroller like any other. Every other ancestor is tested whether or not
+/// it is the node's containing block, so a fixed or absolutely positioned
+/// node outside a clipping ancestor's box reads as clipped: the error is on
+/// the side of saying so. An ancestor's padding box is scaled by the ratio of
+/// its bounding rectangle to its layout size, which is exact only for
+/// translation and scaling by positive factors: `transformed` is true when
+/// the node or any ancestor is rotated (a half turn included), skewed,
+/// reflected (a negative axis in `transform` or `scale`), transformed in 3D
+/// or placed on a motion path, and the caller then reports no position at
+/// all.
+const DOM_NODE_LOCATE: &str = "function(px, py) { \
+    const node = this; \
+    const doc = node.ownerDocument, view = doc.defaultView; \
+    const r = node.getBoundingClientRect(); \
+    const x = r.left + r.width / 2, y = r.top + r.height / 2; \
+    const root = node.getRootNode(); \
+    const probe = (qx, qy) => { \
+        const at = ('elementFromPoint' in root) ? root.elementFromPoint(qx, qy) : null; \
+        return !at ? 'none' : (at === node ? 'target' : (node.contains(at) ? 'descendant' : 'other')); \
